Use the cross product of each point's offset from the first point against the direction from the first point to the second. If any cross product is nonzero, the points are not collinear. This avoids division and handles vertical lines. Values stay within about 2·10^4 per difference, so the products fit in an int, but I use long to be safe.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkStraightLine(int[][] coordinates) {
        long dx = coordinates[1][0] - coordinates[0][0];
        long dy = coordinates[1][1] - coordinates[0][1];
        for (int i = 2; i < coordinates.length; i++) {
            long ex = coordinates[i][0] - coordinates[0][0];
            long ey = coordinates[i][1] - coordinates[0][1];
            if (dx * ey - dy * ex != 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

