inference_trace:
Sort the array, then let `dp[i]` be the number of trees whose root is `arr[i]`. Every number alone is a one-node tree, so `dp[i]` starts at 1. For each `j < i` with `arr[i] % arr[j] == 0`, let `q = arr[i] / arr[j]`. If `q` is in the array, add `dp[j] * dp[idx(q)]` to `dp[i]`. Looping over every valid `j` counts both orderings of the children, which matches the examples, since `[10,2,5]` and `[10,5,2]` are counted separately. The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]` modulo 1e9+7.

Use a `HashMap` from value to index, and `long` arithmetic for the products. This is O(n²), about 10⁶ operations for n = 1000.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int numFactoredBinaryTrees(int[] arr) {
        long MOD = 1000000007L;
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        int n = arr.length;
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) map.put(arr[i], i);
        long[] dp = new long[n];
        long total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = 1;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (arr[i] % arr[j] == 0) {
                    int q = arr[i] / arr[j];
                    Integer k = map.get(q);
                    if (k != null) {
                        dp[i] = (dp[i] + dp[j] * dp[k]) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }
            total = (total + dp[i]) % MOD;
        }
        return (int) total;
    }
}
```

