inference_trace:
This is solved offline by reversing the hits and using union-find with component sizes.

1. Copy the grid. For each hit, if the cell holds a brick, set it to 0. Cells that had no brick are left alone so they are not wrongly restored later.
2. Build a union-find over m·n cells plus a virtual top node at index m·n, tracking component sizes. Union every remaining brick with its right and down neighbours, and union the bricks in row 0 with the top node.
3. Process the hits in reverse. If the original cell had no brick, the answer is 0. Otherwise:
   - Record the top component size `before`.
   - Restore the brick. If its row is 0, union it with the top.
   - Union it with each neighbouring brick.
   - Let `after` be the new top component size. The answer is `max(0, after - before - 1)`, where the 1 is the restored brick itself.

The hits are unique, so the original grid is enough to tell whether a hit cell had a brick. I keep a copy `g` for the modified grid and test `grid[x][y] == 1` on the original.

The size of the top component is `size[find(top)]`. Since the top node has size 1 initially, the virtual node's own size cancels out in `after - before`.

```java
class Solution {
    int[] p, sz;
    int find(int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) {
            p[x] = p[p[x]];
            x = p[x];
        }
        return x;
    }
    void union(int a, int b) {
        a = find(a);
        b = find(b);
        if (a == b) return;
        if (sz[a] < sz[b]) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }
        p[b] = a;
        sz[a] += sz[b];
    }
    public int[] hitBricks(int[][] grid, int[][] hits) {
        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
        int top = m * n;
        p = new int[top + 1];
        sz = new int[top + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= top; i++) { p[i] = i; sz[i] = 1; }
        int[][] g = new int[m][n];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) g[i] = grid[i].clone();
        for (int[] h : hits) g[h[0]][h[1]] = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (g[i][j] == 1) {
                    if (i == 0) union(j, top);
                    if (i > 0 && g[i - 1][j] == 1) union(i * n + j, (i - 1) * n + j);
                    if (j > 0 && g[i][j - 1] == 1) union(i * n + j, i * n + j - 1);
                }
            }
        }
        int[] dx = {1, -1, 0, 0};
        int[] dy = {0, 0, 1, -1};
        int k = hits.length;
        int[] res = new int[k];
        for (int i = k - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int x = hits[i][0], y = hits[i][1];
            if (grid[x][y] == 0) continue;
            int before = sz[find(top)];
            g[x][y] = 1;
            if (x == 0) union(y, top);
            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                int nx = x + dx[d], ny = y + dy[d];
                if (nx >= 0 && ny >= 0 && nx < m && ny < n && g[nx][ny] == 1) {
                    union(x * n + y, nx * n + ny);
                }
            }
            int after = sz[find(top)];
            res[i] = Math.max(0, after - before - 1);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

