Track the index of the previous 1. When a new 1 appears at index `i`, there are `i - prev - 1` zeros between the two. If that count is less than `k`, return false. Otherwise update `prev` and continue. Initialize `prev` to a sentinel meaning "no 1 seen yet", so the first 1 never triggers a failure.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean kLengthApart(int[] nums, int k) {
        int prev = -1;
        boolean seen = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == 1) {
                if (seen && i - prev - 1 < k) return false;
                prev = i;
                seen = true;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

