inference_trace:
The examples show trailing spaces in the strings (e.g. "dcce "), which looks like a formatting artifact. To be safe, `f` should only count lowercase letters and skip anything else. A trailing space has a lower char value than 'a', so it would otherwise be picked as the smallest character.

Approach:
1. Compute `f(W)` for every word. Since each word has length at most 10, `f` is between 1 and 10.
2. Build `cnt[k]`, the number of words with `f = k`, then a suffix sum `suf[k]`, the number of words with `f >= k`.
3. For each query, the answer is `suf[f(q) + 1]`. The array needs size 12 so that index 11 is valid.

```java
class Solution {
    private int f(String s) {
        int[] c = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char ch = s.charAt(i);
            if (ch >= 'a' && ch <= 'z') c[ch - 'a']++;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if (c[i] > 0) return c[i];
        }
        return 0;
    }

    public int[] numSmallerByFrequency(String[] queries, String[] words) {
        int[] suf = new int[13];
        for (String w : words) {
            suf[f(w)]++;
        }
        for (int i = 11; i >= 0; i--) {
            suf[i] += suf[i + 1];
        }
        int[] res = new int[queries.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < queries.length; i++) {
            int fq = f(queries[i]);
            res[i] = suf[Math.min(fq + 1, 12)];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

