Brute force over all pairs `(a, b)` with `1 <= a, b <= n` is enough, since `n <= 250` gives at most about 62,500 pairs. For each pair, compute `a*a + b*b` and check whether it is a perfect square `c*c` with `c <= n`. Each valid ordered pair adds one to the count, which matches the examples (both `(3,4,5)` and `(4,3,5)` are counted).

```java
class Solution {
    public int countTriples(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int a = 1; a <= n; a++) {
            for (int b = 1; b <= n; b++) {
                int s = a * a + b * b;
                int c = (int) Math.round(Math.sqrt(s));
                if (c <= n && c * c == s) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

