Since the length is at most 100, a brute force over all start positions works. For each start `i`, extend the end `j` while the characters are vowels. Keep a bitmask of the vowels seen so far, and whenever the mask equals 31 (all five present), add 1 to the count. Stop extending at the first non-vowel.

The example inputs in the statement have trailing spaces, such as "aeiouu ". A space is not a vowel, so it simply ends the run and does not affect the count.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countVowelSubstrings(String word) {
        int n = word.length();
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int mask = 0;
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int b = "aeiou".indexOf(word.charAt(j));
                if (b < 0) break;
                mask |= 1 << b;
                if (mask == 31) count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

