Greedy approach: keep `lo = 0` and `hi = n`. For each character, if it is `'I'`, take `lo` and increment it. If it is `'D'`, take `hi` and decrement it. After the loop, `lo == hi`, so the last element is `lo`.

This works because choosing the smallest remaining value for `'I'` guarantees the next value is larger, and choosing the largest remaining value for `'D'` guarantees the next value is smaller.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] diStringMatch(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        int[] res = new int[n + 1];
        int lo = 0, hi = n;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == 'I') {
                res[i] = lo++;
            } else {
                res[i] = hi--;
            }
        }
        res[n] = lo;
        return res;
    }
}
```

