Scan the array once, keeping a pointer `last` to the next index not yet checked. For each index `j` where `nums[j] == key`, add every index from `max(last, j-k)` to `min(n-1, j+k)`, then set `last` to one past the end of that range. This avoids duplicates and keeps the output sorted.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> findKDistantIndices(int[] nums, int key, int k) {
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = nums.length;
        int last = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[j] == key) {
                int start = Math.max(last, j - k);
                int end = Math.min(n - 1, j + k);
                for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
                    res.add(i);
                }
                last = Math.max(last, end + 1);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

