A node is safe exactly when no path from it reaches a cycle, so I can find the safe nodes with a topological sort on the reversed graph (Kahn's algorithm).

1. For each node, count its outgoing edges (`outdeg[i] = graph[i].length`).
2. Build the reverse adjacency: for each edge `i -> j`, add `i` to `rev[j]`.
3. Put every node with out-degree 0 (terminal nodes) into a queue.
4. Pop a node and mark it safe. For each predecessor in `rev[node]`, decrement its out-degree. If that reaches 0, all of its successors are safe, so push it.
5. Collect the safe nodes in index order, which gives the ascending result.

Self-loops and cycles keep a node's out-degree above 0 forever, so those nodes never enter the queue. That matches the definition of unsafe.

Imports are needed because the starter snippet doesn't include them; I add `java.util.*` above the class.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.length;
        int[] out = new int[n];
        List<List<Integer>> rev = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) rev.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            out[i] = graph[i].length;
            for (int j : graph[i]) rev.get(j).add(i);
        }
        ArrayDeque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (out[i] == 0) q.add(i);
        boolean[] safe = new boolean[n];
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int u = q.poll();
            safe[u] = true;
            for (int p : rev.get(u)) {
                if (--out[p] == 0) q.add(p);
            }
        }
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (safe[i]) res.add(i);
        return res;
    }
}
```

