Iterate from the end of the string, skipping dashes. Append each remaining character in uppercase to a StringBuilder, and insert a dash whenever `k` characters have been added since the last dash. Then reverse the builder.

Details:
- Keep a counter of characters added since the last dash. Before appending a character, if the counter equals `k`, append `'-'` and reset the counter to 0.
- This never leaves a trailing dash, because a dash is added only when another character follows.
- If every character is a dash, the result is an empty string.

The examples in the statement have a trailing space inside the string (e.g. "5F3Z-2e-9-w "). I treat this as a formatting artifact of the problem statement and not part of the input. If a space really were in `s`, it would be treated as an ordinary character, which is consistent with the expected outputs also ending in a space.

```java
class Solution {
    public String licenseKeyFormatting(String s, int k) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c == '-') continue;
            if (cnt == k) {
                sb.append('-');
                cnt = 0;
            }
            sb.append(Character.toUpperCase(c));
            cnt++;
        }
        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```

