This uses the patience-sorting approach in O(n log n). Keep an array `tails`, where `tails[i]` is the smallest possible tail value of a strictly increasing subsequence of length `i+1`.

For each number, binary search for the first index in `tails` whose value is `>= num`. Replace that entry with `num`, or append it if no such index exists. Searching for `>=` is what makes the subsequence strictly increasing, since equal values replace an existing tail instead of extending it. The final length of `tails` is the answer.

```java
class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int[] tails = new int[nums.length];
        int size = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            int lo = 0, hi = size;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
                if (tails[mid] < num) {
                    lo = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    hi = mid;
                }
            }
            tails[lo] = num;
            if (lo == size) {
                size++;
            }
        }
        return size;
    }
}
```

