inference_trace:
For each point i, count the other points j by the normalized slope of the line through i and j. The largest count for any i, plus one for i itself, is the answer.

To normalize a slope, take dx and dy and divide both by gcd(|dx|, |dy|). Then fix the sign so that dx > 0, or dx == 0 and dy > 0. Vertical lines then get a single key, and so do horizontal ones.

Coordinates are at most 1e4 in absolute value, so dx and dy are at most 2e4 in absolute value. That means the key `dx * 100003L + dy` is unique if dy is shifted to be nonnegative. A simpler option is a `HashMap<Long,Integer>` with key `(long)dx * 100000 + dy`. Since dy lies in [-20000, 20000] and the multiplier is 100000, distinct pairs give distinct keys.

Edge case: if n <= 2, return n.

The cost is O(n²) map operations, about 90k for n = 300, which is fine.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int t = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = t;
        }
        return a;
    }

    public int maxPoints(int[][] points) {
        int n = points.length;
        if (n <= 2) return n;
        int best = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            HashMap<Long, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int dx = points[j][0] - points[i][0];
                int dy = points[j][1] - points[i][1];
                int g = gcd(Math.abs(dx), Math.abs(dy));
                dx /= g;
                dy /= g;
                if (dx < 0 || (dx == 0 && dy < 0)) {
                    dx = -dx;
                    dy = -dy;
                }
                long key = (long) dx * 100000L + dy;
                int c = map.merge(key, 1, Integer::sum);
                if (c + 1 > best) best = c + 1;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

