This is the known problem where operation 1 is "00" -> "10" and operation 2 is "10" -> "01".

Let `first` be the index of the first '0'. If there is no '0', the string is already all ones and is returned as is. Every '1' before `first` stays in place.

From `first` onward, count the zeros as `zeros`. Operation 2 lets zeros move right past ones, so all the zeros can be gathered together. Operation 1 then turns each adjacent "00" into "10", so all but one zero become ones. The single remaining zero ends up at position `first + zeros - 1`, and every other position is '1'.

Check with "000110": `first` = 0 and `zeros` = 4 (positions 0, 1, 2, 5). The zero goes at index 0 + 4 - 1 = 3, giving "111011", which matches the expected output.

Check with "01": `first` = 0 and `zeros` = 1. The zero goes at index 0, giving "01", which matches.

Implementation: find `first`, count zeros from `first` to the end, build a char array filled with '1', and set the zero at `first + zeros - 1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public String maximumBinaryString(String binary) {
        int n = binary.length();
        int first = binary.indexOf('0');
        if (first < 0) return binary;
        int zeros = 0;
        for (int i = first; i < n; i++) {
            if (binary.charAt(i) == '0') zeros++;
        }
        char[] c = new char[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(c, '1');
        c[first + zeros - 1] = '0';
        return new String(c);
    }
}
```

