Track `cnt[x]`, the number of occurrences of each value, and `freq[c]`, the number of values that occur exactly `c` times. Also keep `maxF`, the largest count seen so far. For each prefix of length `i` (1-indexed), check whether removing one element can make all counts equal. This holds in these cases:

1. `maxF == 1`: every value appears once, so removing any one element leaves all counts equal to 1.
2. `freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == i` and `freq[1] == 1`... more precisely, the case where one value occurs once and every other value occurs `maxF` times. Then `freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == i`, and removing the singleton leaves equal counts.
3. `freq[maxF] == 1` and `freq[maxF-1] * (maxF-1) + maxF == i`: one value occurs `maxF` times and all the others occur `maxF-1` times. Removing one occurrence of the top value equalizes them.
4. Only one distinct value: it is covered by case 3 with `freq[maxF-1] = 0`, since `0 + maxF == i`. When `maxF == 1` and `i == 1`, case 1 already applies.

Updating per element x:
- `freq[cnt[x]]--` if `cnt[x] > 0`
- `cnt[x]++`
- `freq[cnt[x]]++`
- `maxF = max(maxF, cnt[x])`

Check on example 1 at `i = 7`: the prefix is 2,2,1,1,5,3,3, so counts are 2,2,1,2 and `maxF = 2`. `freq[2] = 3`, and `3*2 + 1 = 7 == i`, so case 2 holds and the answer is at least 7. At `i = 8` the counts are all 2, `freq[2] = 4`, and `4*2 + 1 = 9 != 8`. Case 3 needs `freq[2] == 1`, which fails. So the answer is 7, as expected.

Check on example 2 at `i = 13`: `maxF = 3`, `freq[3] = 4`, and `4*3 + 1 = 13`, so case 2 holds and the answer is 13.

