Mark the broken letters in a boolean array, then scan `text` once. Count a word as typeable if none of its letters is marked.

The examples show trailing spaces (e.g. "hello world "), so the code should not assume the text ends with a letter. Iterating character by character handles this: on a space (or at the end of the string), the current word is finished, so add it to the count if it had no broken letter. An empty word, such as one after a trailing space, must not be counted, so I track whether the current word has any characters.

The `brokenLetters` string may also contain a space, as in "ad ". Marking a space as broken is harmless, because spaces are treated as separators and never looked up.

Algorithm:
1. Mark each character of `brokenLetters` that is between 'a' and 'z' in a boolean array.
2. Keep `ok = true` and `len = 0`.
3. For each character in `text`:
   - If it is a space: if `len > 0` and `ok`, increment the count. Then reset `ok = true` and `len = 0`.
   - Otherwise: increment `len`, and set `ok = false` if the letter is broken.
4. After the loop, apply the same check once more for the final word.

```java
class Solution {
    public int canBeTypedWords(String text, String brokenLetters) {
        boolean[] broken = new boolean[26];
        for (char c : brokenLetters.toCharArray()) {
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') broken[c - 'a'] = true;
        }
        int count = 0;
        boolean ok = true;
        int len = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < text.length(); i++) {
            char c = text.charAt(i);
            if (c == ' ') {
                if (len > 0 && ok) count++;
                ok = true;
                len = 0;
            } else {
                len++;
                if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' && broken[c - 'a']) ok = false;
            }
        }
        if (len > 0 && ok) count++;
        return count;
    }
}
```

