- Sort `initial` so that ties go to the smallest index. Or just compare on equal count and smaller index.
- For each removed node r, mark r as blocked. Start the BFS from every other initial node. Neighbors are found through the adjacency matrix, skipping r.
- The count is the number of visited nodes. Keep the best (smallest count, then smallest index).

The removed node is never visited, so it is not counted. It also cannot spread infection, since it is blocked and never enqueued.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int minMalwareSpread(int[][] graph, int[] initial) {
        int n = graph.length;
        int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int ans = -1;
        for (int r : initial) {
            boolean[] vis = new boolean[n];
            vis[r] = true;
            int[] queue = new int[n];
            int head = 0, tail = 0;
            int count = 0;
            for (int s : initial) {
                if (s == r) continue;
                if (!vis[s]) {
                    vis[s] = true;
                    queue[tail++] = s;
                    count++;
                }
            }
            while (head < tail) {
                int u = queue[head++];
                for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {
                    if (graph[u][v] == 1 && !vis[v]) {
                        vis[v] = true;
                        count++;
                        queue[tail++] = v;
                    }
                }
            }
            if (count < best || (count == best && r < ans)) {
                best = count;
                ans = r;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

