Approach: put all points in a hash set, then check every pair of points as opposite (diagonal) corners of a rectangle. For each pair with different x and different y, look up the other two corners in the set. With 500 points that is about 125k pairs, which is fast.

Encoding: key = x * 40001L + y, stored in a `HashSet<Long>`.

For each pair i < j, skip it if x1 == x2 or y1 == y2. Otherwise, if both (x1, y2) and (x2, y1) are in the set, update the minimum with |x1-x2| * |y1-y2|. The area is at most 4e4 * 4e4 = 1.6e9, which fits in an int (max about 2.147e9), but I use long for the computation and cast at the end to be safe.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int minAreaRect(int[][] points) {
        HashSet<Long> set = new HashSet<>();
        for (int[] p : points) {
            set.add(p[0] * 40001L + p[1]);
        }
        long best = Long.MAX_VALUE;
        int n = points.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int x1 = points[i][0], y1 = points[i][1];
                int x2 = points[j][0], y2 = points[j][1];
                if (x1 == x2 || y1 == y2) continue;
                long area = Math.abs((long) (x1 - x2)) * Math.abs((long) (y1 - y2));
                if (area >= best) continue;
                if (set.contains(x1 * 40001L + y2) && set.contains(x2 * 40001L + y1)) {
                    best = area;
                }
            }
        }
        return best == Long.MAX_VALUE ? 0 : (int) best;
    }
}
```

