I use an iterative BFS by level, which avoids stack overflow on a skewed tree of up to 10^5 nodes. The first leaf reached is the nearest one, so its level is the minimum depth. An empty tree returns 0.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int minDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        Queue<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        q.add(root);
        int depth = 1;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode n = q.poll();
                if (n.left == null && n.right == null) return depth;
                if (n.left != null) q.add(n.left);
                if (n.right != null) q.add(n.right);
            }
            depth++;
        }
        return depth;
    }
}
```

