The answer is always 0, 1, or 2, so I check each case in turn.

1. If the grid already has a number of islands other than 1 (zero or several), return 0.
2. Otherwise, try removing each land cell in turn and recount the islands. If any removal leaves a count other than 1, return 1.
3. If no single removal works, return 2.

Why 2 is always enough: any island has a corner cell with at most 2 land neighbors, so removing those neighbors isolates it. A single land cell is handled by step 2, since removing it leaves 0 islands.

Cost: there are at most 900 cells, and each removal triggers a flood fill of at most 900 cells, so the total is about 810k operations, which is fine.

For the island count, I use an iterative DFS with a visited array and a stack, which avoids recursion depth issues.

```java
class Solution {
    int m, n;

    int count(int[][] g) {
        boolean[][] vis = new boolean[m][n];
        int c = 0;
        int[] dx = {1, -1, 0, 0};
        int[] dy = {0, 0, 1, -1};
        int[] stack = new int[m * n];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (g[i][j] == 1 && !vis[i][j]) {
                    c++;
                    int sp = 0;
                    stack[sp++] = i * n + j;
                    vis[i][j] = true;
                    while (sp > 0) {
                        int cur = stack[--sp];
                        int x = cur / n, y = cur % n;
                        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                            int nx = x + dx[d], ny = y + dy[d];
                            if (nx >= 0 && ny >= 0 && nx < m && ny < n && g[nx][ny] == 1 && !vis[nx][ny]) {
                                vis[nx][ny] = true;
                                stack[sp++] = nx * n + ny;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return c;
    }

    public int minDays(int[][] grid) {
        m = grid.length;
        n = grid[0].length;
        if (count(grid) != 1) return 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    grid[i][j] = 0;
                    int c = count(grid);
                    grid[i][j] = 1;
                    if (c != 1) return 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return 2;
    }
}
```

