Binary search on the number of days. First, if `m * k > n`, there are not enough flowers, so return -1. Use a `long` for the product, since `m` can reach 10^6 and `k` up to 10^5.

Otherwise, search between the minimum and maximum values in `bloomDay`. For a candidate day `d`, scan the garden and keep a count of consecutive flowers with `bloomDay[i] <= d`. When the count reaches `k`, form one bouquet and reset the count to 0. If the count is broken by an unbloomed flower, reset it to 0. The candidate `d` is feasible if the bouquet total reaches `m`.

Feasibility is monotonic in `d`, so the smallest feasible day is found by the standard lower-bound binary search.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minDays(int[] bloomDay, int m, int k) {
        int n = bloomDay.length;
        if ((long) m * k > n) return -1;
        int lo = Integer.MAX_VALUE, hi = 0;
        for (int b : bloomDay) {
            lo = Math.min(lo, b);
            hi = Math.max(hi, b);
        }
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int count = 0, bouquets = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (bloomDay[i] <= mid) {
                    count++;
                    if (count == k) {
                        bouquets++;
                        count = 0;
                    }
                } else {
                    count = 0;
                }
            }
            if (bouquets >= m) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
}
```

