inference_trace:
Use a bitmask DP over subsets of tasks. Let `dp[mask]` be the pair (sessions used, time used in the last session), minimized lexicographically.

Start with `dp[0] = (1, 0)`. For each mask and each task `i` not in it, try adding `i`:
- If `time + tasks[i] <= sessionTime`, the new state is `(sessions, time + tasks[i])`.
- Otherwise the new state is `(sessions + 1, tasks[i])`.

Keep the lexicographically smaller pair. This greedy choice is valid because fewer sessions is always at least as good, and with equal sessions, less time used in the last session is better.

To compare pairs cheaply, encode each as a single integer `sessions * 100 + time`. Since time is at most 15, this keeps the order correct. For a new task, decode `s = v / 100` and `t = v % 100`, compute the new pair, and take the minimum. The answer is `dp[full] / 100`.

With n = 14 this is 2^14 · 14 operations, which is trivial.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minSessions(int[] tasks, int sessionTime) {
        int n = tasks.length;
        int full = (1 << n) - 1;
        int[] dp = new int[1 << n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        dp[0] = 100;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) continue;
            int s = dp[mask] / 100;
            int t = dp[mask] % 100;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                int ns, nt;
                if (t + tasks[i] <= sessionTime) {
                    ns = s;
                    nt = t + tasks[i];
                } else {
                    ns = s + 1;
                    nt = tasks[i];
                }
                int v = ns * 100 + nt;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (v < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = v;
            }
        }
        return dp[full] / 100;
    }
}
```

