Use a monotonic stack of indices and iterate over the array twice (indices 0 to 2n-1, taking `i % n`).

For each position, while the stack is non-empty and the element at the top index is smaller than the current element, pop it and set its answer to the current element. During the first pass (`i < n`), push the current index. Every answer starts at -1, so any index that is never popped keeps -1.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] res = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(res, -1);
        int[] stack = new int[n];
        int top = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            int v = nums[i % n];
            while (top > 0 && nums[stack[top - 1]] < v) {
                res[stack[--top]] = v;
            }
            if (i < n) {
                stack[top++] = i;
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

