Since n ≤ 10^6, the answer is at most 1224444 (the smallest balanced number above 10^6), so a simple brute-force scan is enough.

Start at n+1 and check each number. For each candidate, count its digits. The number is balanced if every digit d that appears has a count of exactly d. A digit 0 can never be balanced, because any occurrence gives a count greater than 0. The check handles this naturally: count[0] > 0 means the number fails.

The scan runs at most about 1.2 million iterations, each with about 7 digit operations, which is fast.

```java
class Solution {
    public int nextBeautifulNumber(int n) {
        for (int x = n + 1; ; x++) {
            int[] c = new int[10];
            int y = x;
            while (y > 0) {
                c[y % 10]++;
                y /= 10;
            }
            boolean ok = true;
            for (int d = 0; d < 10; d++) {
                if (c[d] != 0 && c[d] != d) {
                    ok = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (ok) return x;
        }
    }
}
```

