inference_trace:
The count is computed recursively. For a list, the first element is the root. Split the rest into the elements smaller than the root (left) and larger (right), keeping their original order. The number of orderings is C(l+r, l) · ways(left) · ways(right). The final answer is that count minus 1, since the original ordering is excluded.

Binomials come from Pascal's triangle mod p, a 1001×1001 table. This is cheap for n ≤ 1000.

The recursion depth can reach 1000, which is fine. Each level builds new lists, so the total work is O(n²) in the worst case, which is acceptable.

Implementation: `ways(List<Integer>)` returns a long. If the size is ≤ 2, it returns 1. Otherwise it splits around the root, recurses on both parts, and combines them with `C[l+r][l]`.

Check on example 2, [3,4,5,1,2]: the root is 3, left = [1,2], right = [4,5]. C(4,2) = 6, and each side has 1 way, so the total is 6. Subtracting 1 gives 5, which matches.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    static final long MOD = 1000000007L;
    long[][] C;

    public int numOfWays(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        C = new long[n + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            C[i][0] = 1;
            for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
                C[i][j] = (C[i - 1][j - 1] + (j <= i - 1 ? C[i - 1][j] : 0)) % MOD;
            }
        }
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int x : nums) list.add(x);
        long res = ways(list);
        return (int) (((res - 1) % MOD + MOD) % MOD);
    }

    private long ways(List<Integer> list) {
        int sz = list.size();
        if (sz <= 2) return 1;
        int root = list.get(0);
        List<Integer> left = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> right = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 1; i < sz; i++) {
            int x = list.get(i);
            if (x < root) left.add(x);
            else right.add(x);
        }
        long a = ways(left);
        long b = ways(right);
        long r = C[left.size() + right.size()][left.size()];
        return r * a % MOD * b % MOD;
    }
}
```

