For each type, count pairs (j<k) in one array by their product using a HashMap<Long,Integer>. This takes O(n²) time, at most about 500k pairs per array. Then for each element in the other array, look up its square in the map.

Products reach 1e10, so they need long keys. The squares are also up to 1e10, so use long there too.

Implementation: a helper `count(a, b)` builds a map of products of pairs from b, then sums `map.get((long) x * x)` over x in a. The answer is `count(nums1, nums2) + count(nums2, nums1)`.

The result is at most about 1000 × 500k = 5e8, which fits in an int. In the worst case, with all ones, type 1 gives 1000 × C(1000,2) ≈ 5e8 and type 2 the same, so the total is about 1e9. That is still under 2.147e9, so int is fine. I'll use long internally and cast at the end anyway.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int numTriplets(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
        return (int) (count(nums1, nums2) + count(nums2, nums1));
    }

    private long count(int[] a, int[] b) {
        HashMap<Long, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int j = 0; j < b.length; j++) {
            for (int k = j + 1; k < b.length; k++) {
                long p = (long) b[j] * b[k];
                map.merge(p, 1, Integer::sum);
            }
        }
        long res = 0;
        for (int x : a) {
            Integer c = map.get((long) x * x);
            if (c != null) res += c;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

