inference_trace:
Use a union-find structure. For each request, find the roots of u and v. If the roots are equal, the two are already in the same component, so the request succeeds. Otherwise, check every restriction (x, y): if the roots of x and y are the same as the roots of u and v in either order, the request fails. If no restriction blocks it, union the two components and mark the request successful.

The cost is O(requests × restrictions) = 10^6 find operations, which is fine.

```java
class Solution {
    int[] p;
    int find(int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) {
            p[x] = p[p[x]];
            x = p[x];
        }
        return x;
    }
    public boolean[] friendRequests(int n, int[][] restrictions, int[][] requests) {
        p = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i] = i;
        boolean[] res = new boolean[requests.length];
        for (int j = 0; j < requests.length; j++) {
            int a = find(requests[j][0]);
            int b = find(requests[j][1]);
            if (a == b) {
                res[j] = true;
                continue;
            }
            boolean ok = true;
            for (int[] r : restrictions) {
                int x = find(r[0]);
                int y = find(r[1]);
                if ((x == a && y == b) || (x == b && y == a)) {
                    ok = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (ok) {
                p[a] = b;
                res[j] = true;
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

