This uses reservoir sampling. The constructor stores the array. On each `pick`, scan the array and count the indices where `nums[i] == target`. For the k-th match, replace the chosen index with probability 1/k, which is `rnd.nextInt(k) == 0`. This gives every matching index an equal chance, and it needs no extra memory beyond the array.

Cost: each `pick` is O(n), so 10^4 calls on 2·10^4 elements is about 2·10^8 simple operations in the worst case, which is acceptable.

```java
import java.util.Random;

class Solution {
    private int[] nums;
    private Random rnd;

    public Solution(int[] nums) {
        this.nums = nums;
        this.rnd = new Random();
    }

    public int pick(int target) {
        int count = 0;
        int res = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == target) {
                count++;
                if (rnd.nextInt(count) == 0) {
                    res = i;
                }
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}

