Split the string on whitespace to get the day, month and year parts. Take the digits from the day token by dropping its last two characters (the suffix). Look up the month's index from a list of month abbreviations. Format the result as `YYYY-MM-DD`.

The examples show trailing spaces inside the quotes, which are probably formatting artifacts. The real input is likely "20th Oct 2052" with no trailing space. If a trailing space were present, `trim()` followed by `split("\\s+")` would still handle it. The expected output in the examples also shows a trailing space, but that is probably an artifact too, so I return the string without one.

```java
class Solution {
    public String reformatDate(String date) {
        String[] p = date.trim().split("\\s+");
        String d = p[0].substring(0, p[0].length() - 2);
        if (d.length() == 1) d = "0" + d;
        String[] months = {"Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"};
        int m = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
            if (months[i].equals(p[1])) {
                m = i + 1;
                break;
            }
        }
        String mm = m < 10 ? "0" + m : "" + m;
        return p[2] + "-" + mm + "-" + d;
    }
}
```

