This is a standard dynamic programming problem. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when the first `i` characters of `s` match the first `j` characters of `p`.

**Base case:** `dp[0][0] = true`.

**Transitions for `j` from 1 to `n`, `i` from 0 to `m`:**
- If `p[j-1] == '*'`, the starred element is `p[j-2]`.
  - Zero occurrences: `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2]`.
  - One or more occurrences: if `i > 0` and `p[j-2]` is `'.'` or equals `s[i-1]`, then `dp[i][j] |= dp[i-1][j]`.
- Otherwise, `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]` when `i > 0` and `p[j-1]` is `'.'` or equals `s[i-1]`.

The row `i = 0` is handled by the same loop, since the zero-occurrence case lets patterns like `a*b*` match the empty string.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        dp[0][0] = true;
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);
                if (pc == '*') {
                    if (j >= 2) {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2];
                        char prev = p.charAt(j - 2);
                        if (!dp[i][j] && i > 0 && (prev == '.' || prev == s.charAt(i - 1))) {
                            dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    if (i > 0 && (pc == '.' || pc == s.charAt(i - 1))) {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }
}
