I'll use the standard approach: count the minimum number of left and right parentheses to remove, then run a DFS that skips duplicates.

1. Scan the string. For each `(`, increment `open`. For each `)`, decrement `open` if it is positive, otherwise increment `rightRem`. At the end, `leftRem = open`.
2. Run a DFS over the string with the state `(index, leftRem, rightRem, count, current string)`, where `count` is the number of currently unmatched `(` in the built string.
3. At each character:
   - If it is `(` or `)`, first try removing it. To avoid duplicates, skip the removal when the previous character is the same parenthesis. This works because from a run of identical parentheses we only remove the first ones in the run.
   - Then try keeping it. Keeping `(` increments `count`. Keeping `)` is only allowed if `count > 0`, and it decrements `count`.
   - Letters are always kept.
4. At the end of the string, if `leftRem == 0`, `rightRem == 0` and `count == 0`, add the string to the result.

The duplicate-skip rule is: if `i > 0` and `s[i] == s[i-1]`, don't branch into removing `s[i]`. This is the standard approach, but it only works correctly when the removal of a run is always a prefix of the run. Keeping the first character of a run and removing a later one would give the same string as removing the first and keeping the later one. The rule instead forbids removing a character whose predecessor was kept. Predecessor-kept tracking is therefore required, so the simple version isn't enough.

To avoid that, I use a `HashSet` for the results, which is safe with at most 20 parentheses, and prune on `leftRem`, `rightRem` and `count`. The removal branch requires `leftRem > 0` for `(` or `rightRem > 0` for `)`. The set guarantees uniqueness.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private Set<String> res = new HashSet<>();
    private String s;

    public List<String> removeInvalidParentheses(String s) {
        this.s = s;
        int open = 0, rightRem = 0;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '(') open++;
            else if (c == ')') {
                if (open > 0) open--;
                else rightRem++;
            }
        }
        dfs(0, open, rightRem, 0, new StringBuilder());
        return new ArrayList<>(res);
    }

    private void dfs(int i, int l, int r, int count, StringBuilder sb) {
        if (count < 0) return;
        if (i == s.length()) {
            if (l == 0 && r == 0 && count == 0) res.add(sb.toString());
            return;
        }
        char c = s.charAt(i);
        if (c == '(') {
            if (l > 0) dfs(i + 1, l - 1, r, count, sb);
            sb.append(c);
            dfs(i + 1, l, r, count + 1, sb);
            sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
        } else if (c == ')') {
            if (r > 0) dfs(i + 1, l, r - 1, count, sb);
            if (count > 0) {
                sb.append(c);
                dfs(i + 1, l, r, count - 1, sb);
                sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
            }
        } else {
            sb.append(c);
            dfs(i + 1, l, r, count, sb);
            sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

