Approach: memoized recursion over (i, j, len), where the state asks whether s1[i..i+len) can be scrambled into s2[j..j+len).

Base cases:
- If the two substrings are equal, return true.
- If their character counts differ, return false. This prune also keeps the recursion cheap.

Otherwise, try each split point k from 1 to len-1:
- No swap: `s1[i..i+k)` matches `s2[j..j+k)` and `s1[i+k..)` matches `s2[j+k..)`.
- Swap: `s1[i..i+k)` matches `s2[j+len-k..)` and `s1[i+k..)` matches `s2[j..j+len-k)`.

The memo is a `byte[31][30][30]` array, indexed by length, i and j, where 0 means not computed, 1 means true and 2 means false.

For the character count check, compare `int[26]` counts built over the substrings. The cost is O(len) per state, and there are about 30^3 states, which is fine.

```java
class Solution {
    String a, b;
    byte[][][] memo;

    public boolean isScramble(String s1, String s2) {
        a = s1;
        b = s2;
        int n = s1.length();
        memo = new byte[n + 1][n][n];
        return solve(0, 0, n);
    }

    boolean solve(int i, int j, int len) {
        if (memo[len][i][j] != 0) return memo[len][i][j] == 1;
        boolean res = false;
        if (a.regionMatches(i, b, j, len)) {
            res = true;
        } else {
            int[] cnt = new int[26];
            for (int t = 0; t < len; t++) {
                cnt[a.charAt(i + t) - 'a']++;
                cnt[b.charAt(j + t) - 'a']--;
            }
            boolean ok = true;
            for (int c : cnt) if (c != 0) { ok = false; break; }
            if (ok) {
                for (int k = 1; k < len && !res; k++) {
                    if (solve(i, j, k) && solve(i + k, j + k, len - k)) res = true;
                    else if (solve(i, j + len - k, k) && solve(i + k, j, len - k)) res = true;
                }
            }
        }
        memo[len][i][j] = (byte) (res ? 1 : 2);
        return res;
    }
}
```

