Sort the products, then for each prefix of `searchWord` keep a window `[lo, hi]` of products that still match. As each character is typed, move `lo` forward while the product at `lo` fails to match, and move `hi` backward while the product at `hi` fails to match. The first three products in the window are the suggestions.

A product matches at position `i` when its length exceeds `i` and its character at `i` equals `searchWord.charAt(i)`. Because the array is sorted, the window shrinks monotonically and stays contiguous. This works because every product in the window already shares the first `i` characters with the search word.

Each pointer moves at most `n` steps in total, so the pointer work is O(n + m). Sorting costs O(n log n) comparisons, each up to the product length. Collecting up to three items per prefix is O(m).

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces inside the strings (for example `"mouse "`), but that does not change the logic.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> suggestedProducts(String[] products, String searchWord) {
        Arrays.sort(products);
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        int lo = 0, hi = products.length - 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < searchWord.length(); i++) {
            char c = searchWord.charAt(i);
            while (lo <= hi && (products[lo].length() <= i || products[lo].charAt(i) != c)) lo++;
            while (lo <= hi && (products[hi].length() <= i || products[hi].charAt(i) != c)) hi--;
            List<String> cur = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int j = lo; j <= hi && j < lo + 3; j++) cur.add(products[j]);
            res.add(cur);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

