Treat the grid as a flattened array of length m·n. A shift by k moves the element at flat index p to index (p + k) mod (m·n). So for each new position, I compute which old element lands there and fill the result row by row.

For each flat index p in 0..m·n−1, the new value at p is the old value at (p − k) mod total, where the modulo is made non-negative. The old index q maps back to row q / n and column q % n.

The code uses `java.util.*`, so I include the import.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> shiftGrid(int[][] grid, int k) {
        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
        int total = m * n;
        int s = k % total;
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            List<Integer> row = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                int p = i * n + j;
                int q = ((p - s) % total + total) % total;
                row.add(grid[q / n][q % n]);
            }
            res.add(row);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

