This is a bitmask DP over which characters of the target are already covered.

Let `dp[mask]` be the minimum number of stickers needed to cover the characters in `mask`, with `dp[0] = 0`.

For each reachable mask, find the lowest unset bit `i`. Some sticker must cover that character, so only stickers containing `target[i]` need to be tried. This prunes the search and avoids counting the same set of stickers in different orders.

For each such sticker, copy its letter counts and greedily cover target positions j that are not yet in the mask and whose letter still has a remaining count. This gives `newMask`, and we update `dp[newMask] = min(dp[newMask], dp[mask] + 1)`.

Masks are processed in increasing order, which is valid because `newMask` is always greater than `mask`. The sticker covers at least bit `i`, so the mask strictly grows.

Sticker letter counts are precomputed. The answer is `dp[full]`, or -1 if it is unreachable.

Complexity is about 2^15 · 50 · (26 + 15), which is fine.

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces, but the constraints say lowercase letters only. A space in a sticker is never a target letter, so it does no harm. A space in the target could never be covered, and I treat the input as it is without special handling. The counts array is sized 26, so I skip any character outside `a`–`z` when counting sticker letters. If the target contains a non-letter, the index would be invalid, so I use an array of 128 to be safe.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minStickers(String[] stickers, String target) {
        int n = stickers.length;
        int[][] cnt = new int[n][128];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (char c : stickers[i].toCharArray()) {
                cnt[i][c]++;
            }
        }
        int m = target.length();
        int full = (1 << m) - 1;
        int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE / 2;
        int[] dp = new int[1 << m];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, INF);
        dp[0] = 0;
        char[] t = target.toCharArray();
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] >= INF) continue;
            int i = 0;
            while (((mask >> i) & 1) == 1) i++;
            for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {
                if (cnt[s][t[i]] == 0) continue;
                int[] rem = cnt[s].clone();
                int nm = mask;
                for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                    if (((nm >> j) & 1) == 0 && rem[t[j]] > 0) {
                        rem[t[j]]--;
                        nm |= 1 << j;
                    }
                }
                if (dp[mask] + 1 < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = dp[mask] + 1;
            }
        }
        return dp[full] >= INF ? -1 : dp[full];
    }
}
```

