inference_trace:
Approach: build a trie of the reversed words, and keep the stream history in a buffer. On each query, walk the trie from the newest character backwards through the history. Return true as soon as a node marks the end of a word, and stop when no matching child exists. The walk is at most 200 steps, since that is the maximum word length.

The examples show words with trailing spaces (an artifact of formatting), but the constraints say lowercase letters only, so I use 26 children per node.

Implementation details:
- Trie nodes hold `children[26]` and an `end` flag.
- Insert each word reversed.
- Store the stream in a `StringBuilder` or a growable char array. At most 4·10^4 calls are made, so a `StringBuilder` is fine.
- Track the maximum word length so the walk is bounded, although the walk already stops when the trie path ends.

```java
class StreamChecker {

    private static class Node {
        Node[] ch = new Node[26];
        boolean end;
    }

    private Node root = new Node();
    private StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    private int maxLen = 0;

    public StreamChecker(String[] words) {
        for (String w : words) {
            w = w.trim();
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, w.length());
            Node cur = root;
            for (int i = w.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                int c = w.charAt(i) - 'a';
                if (c < 0 || c >= 26) continue;
                if (cur.ch[c] == null) cur.ch[c] = new Node();
                cur = cur.ch[c];
            }
            cur.end = true;
        }
    }

    public boolean query(char letter) {
        sb.append(letter);
        Node cur = root;
        int steps = 0;
        for (int i = sb.length() - 1; i >= 0 && steps < maxLen; i--, steps++) {
            int c = sb.charAt(i) - 'a';
            if (c < 0 || c >= 26) return false;
            cur = cur.ch[c];
            if (cur == null) return false;
            if (cur.end) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

