Greedy approach: at each step, pick the letter with more remaining, unless the last two characters are both that letter, in which case pick the other.

At each step:
- If the last two characters are equal (say 'a'), we must place 'b'.
- Otherwise, place the letter with more remaining count.

Since a valid string is guaranteed to exist, this greedy works. It is the standard solution.

Implementation: loop while a > 0 or b > 0. Let n be the current length of the builder. Check whether the last two characters are both 'a' or both 'b'.
- If they are both 'a', write 'b'.
- If they are both 'b', write 'a'.
- Otherwise, write 'a' if a >= b, else 'b'.

Decrement the matching count. In the forced case the other count must be positive; the guarantee of existence ensures this.

```java
class Solution {
    public String strWithout3a3b(int a, int b) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (a > 0 || b > 0) {
            int n = sb.length();
            boolean writeA;
            if (n >= 2 && sb.charAt(n - 1) == sb.charAt(n - 2)) {
                writeA = sb.charAt(n - 1) == 'b';
            } else {
                writeA = a >= b;
            }
            if (writeA) {
                sb.append('a');
                a--;
            } else {
                sb.append('b');
                b--;
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

