inference_trace:
Count the soldiers in each row, then sort the row indices by (soldier count, index) and return the first k. Since the soldiers are all on the left, a binary search per row would also work, but with m, n ≤ 100 a linear count is fast enough.

To avoid sorting boxed objects, I encode each row as `count * 1000 + index` (the index is below 100, so 1000 is safe). Sorting these integers orders rows by count first and index second. The index for the answer is `key % 1000`.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int[] kWeakestRows(int[][] mat, int k) {
        int m = mat.length;
        int[] keys = new int[m];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            int c = 0;
            for (int v : mat[i]) c += v;
            keys[i] = c * 1000 + i;
        }
        Arrays.sort(keys);
        int[] res = new int[k];
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) res[i] = keys[i] % 1000;
        return res;
    }
}
```

