inference_trace:
Since values are at most 50, I run a DFS and keep, for each value v in 1..50, the most recent (deepest) ancestor on the current path with that value, along with its depth. For each node, I check all 50 values that are coprime with the node's value and take the candidate with the greatest depth.

With n up to 1e5, a recursive DFS could overflow the stack, so I use an iterative one.

Per-value stacks: `nodeAt[v]` is the current node for value v and `depthAt[v]` is its depth. On entering a node, I save the previous entries for its value, set the new ones, and restore them on exit. Each node needs to remember its saved previous node and depth, so I store `prevNode[u]` and `prevDepth[u]`.

Iterative DFS:
- Stack of nodes plus an edge-index pointer per node, using adjacency arrays (head/next).
- On push of u (with parent p and depth d):
  - Compute ans[u] by scanning values 1..50: if `nodeAt[v] != -1` and gcd(v, nums[u]) == 1, take the one with the max depth.
  - Save `prevNode[u] = nodeAt[nums[u]]` and `prevDepth[u] = depthAt[nums[u]]`.
  - Set `nodeAt[nums[u]] = u` and `depthAt[nums[u]] = d`.
- On pop of u, restore `nodeAt[nums[u]] = prevNode[u]` and `depthAt[nums[u]] = prevDepth[u]`.

The answer is computed before u is registered, so a node is never its own ancestor. The coprimality of the 50×50 value pairs is precomputed in a boolean table.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] getCoprimes(int[] nums, int[][] edges) {
        int n = nums.length;
        boolean[][] cop = new boolean[51][51];
        for (int i = 1; i <= 50; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= 50; j++)
                cop[i][j] = gcd(i, j) == 1;
        int[] head = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(head, -1);
        int[] nxt = new int[2 * (n - 1) + 1];
        int[] to = new int[2 * (n - 1) + 1];
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            to[cnt] = e[1]; nxt[cnt] = head[e[0]]; head[e[0]] = cnt++;
            to[cnt] = e[0]; nxt[cnt] = head[e[1]]; head[e[1]] = cnt++;
        }
        int[] ans = new int[n];
        int[] nodeAt = new int[51];
        int[] depthAt = new int[51];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(nodeAt, -1);
        int[] prevNode = new int[n];
        int[] prevDepth = new int[n];
        int[] parent = new int[n];
        int[] depth = new int[n];
        int[] ptr = new int[n];
        int[] stack = new int[n];
        int sp = 0;
        // push root
        parent[0] = -1;
        depth[0] = 0;
        ans[0] = -1;
        prevNode[0] = nodeAt[nums[0]];
        prevDepth[0] = depthAt[nums[0]];
        nodeAt[nums[0]] = 0;
        depthAt[nums[0]] = 0;
        ptr[0] = head[0];
        stack[sp++] = 0;
        while (sp > 0) {
            int u = stack[sp - 1];
            if (ptr[u] == -1) {
                sp--;
                nodeAt[nums[u]] = prevNode[u];
                depthAt[nums[u]] = prevDepth[u];
                continue;
            }
            int e = ptr[u];
            ptr[u] = nxt[e];
            int w = to[e];
            if (w == parent[u]) continue;
            parent[w] = u;
            depth[w] = depth[u] + 1;
            int best = -1, bd = -1;
            for (int v = 1; v <= 50; v++) {
                if (nodeAt[v] != -1 && cop[v][nums[w]] && depthAt[v] > bd) {
                    bd = depthAt[v];
                    best = nodeAt[v];
                }
            }
            ans[w] = best;
            prevNode[w] = nodeAt[nums[w]];
            prevDepth[w] = depthAt[nums[w]];
            nodeAt[nums[w]] = w;
            depthAt[nums[w]] = depth[w];
            ptr[w] = head[w];
            stack[sp++] = w;
        }
        return ans;
    }

    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b);
    }
}
```

