Approach: compute each node's in-degree. Every node must have in-degree at most 1, and exactly one node (the root) must have in-degree 0. Then run a traversal from that root and check that it reaches all n nodes.

1. For each child that is not -1, increment its in-degree. If it becomes greater than 1, return false.
2. Count the nodes with in-degree 0. If the count is not exactly 1, return false.
3. Do a BFS from the root, counting visited nodes. Because every in-degree is at most 1, no node is enqueued twice, so cycles are not an issue. The traversal can only fail by not reaching every node, so a final count equal to n confirms validity.

