inference_trace:
This uses the greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the most recent `*`.

1. Walk through `s` with index `i` and through `p` with index `j`. Also keep `star`, the index of the last `*` seen in `p` (initially -1), and `mark`, the position in `s` where that `*` started matching.
2. If `j < p.length()` and `p[j]` is `?` or equals `s[i]`, advance both `i` and `j`.
3. Otherwise, if `p[j]` is `*`, record `star = j` and `mark = i`, then advance `j` only. The `*` starts out matching the empty sequence.
4. Otherwise, if a previous `*` exists, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `mark`, and set `i = mark`. This makes the `*` absorb one more character of `s`.
5. Otherwise there is no match, so return false.
6. After `s` is consumed, skip any trailing `*` in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` has then reached the end of `p`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, mark = 0;
        int n = s.length(), m = p.length();
        while (i < n) {
            if (j < m && (p.charAt(j) == '?' || p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i))) {
                i++;
                j++;
            } else if (j < m && p.charAt(j) == '*') {
                star = j;
                mark = i;
                j++;
            } else if (star != -1) {
                j = star + 1;
                mark++;
                i = mark;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while (j < m && p.charAt(j) == '*') j++;
        return j == m;
    }
}
```

