Approach: breadth-first search from `beginWord`. For each word, try changing each character to every letter from 'a' to 'z', and keep the result if it is in the dictionary set. Removing a word from the set when it is first reached marks it visited. The answer is the BFS level at which `endWord` is reached, counting words, so the start has level 1. If `endWord` is never reached (including when it is not in `wordList`), return 0.

The cost is about 5000 words × 10 positions × 26 letters, which is fast enough.

The starter has no imports, so I use fully qualified names (`java.util.*`) to be safe. The method signature uses `List`, which the judge provides.

```java
class Solution {
    public int ladderLength(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        java.util.Set<String> dict = new java.util.HashSet<>(wordList);
        if (!dict.contains(endWord)) return 0;
        java.util.Queue<String> q = new java.util.LinkedList<>();
        q.add(beginWord);
        dict.remove(beginWord);
        int level = 1;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            for (int s = 0; s < size; s++) {
                String w = q.poll();
                if (w.equals(endWord)) return level;
                char[] c = w.toCharArray();
                for (int i = 0; i < c.length; i++) {
                    char orig = c[i];
                    for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
                        if (ch == orig) continue;
                        c[i] = ch;
                        String nw = new String(c);
                        if (dict.remove(nw)) {
                            q.add(nw);
                        }
                    }
                    c[i] = orig;
                }
            }
            level++;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```

